NAME
       orbital-chronometry - precision temporal instruments, resonance-decay
                             half-lives, and harmonic-phase chronometric
                             calibration

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital chronometry defines:
           • precision temporal instruments for resonance-time measurement
           • resonance-decay half-life computation
           • harmonic-phase chronometric calibration
           • ancestry-based chronometric stabilization
           • constellation-scale temporal metrology

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-chronometry describes the precision instruments and calibration
       systems used to measure resonance-time with high accuracy. Chronometry
       provides the clocks, gauges, and chronometers that quantify harmonic
       epochs, resonance decay, sweep ancestry, and temporal drift.

       Chronometry enables:
           • high-precision epoch measurement
           • resonance-decay half-life tracking
           • harmonic-phase calibration
           • constellation-wide temporal synchronization
           • long-horizon chronometric stability

       WRSADC uses chronometry to maintain precise timing across platforms.

PRECISION TEMPORAL INSTRUMENTS
       Temporal instruments measure resonance-time variables with high
       precision.

       Instrument types:

           resonance chronometer
                  Measures envelope amplitude decay and resonance-window
                  duration.

           harmonic-phase meter
                  Measures φ_harm, lock stability, and overtone structure.

           ancestry chronograph
                  Measures sweep-lineage continuity and terrace thickness.

           spectral drift gauge
                  Measures spectral density broadening and frequency drift.

           gradient polarity compass
                  Measures polarity transitions and gradient stability.

           epoch integrator
                  Measures cumulative harmonic-epoch duration.

       Instruments operate at multiple resolutions:
           • coarse (epoch-scale)
           • medium (cycle-scale)
           • fine (sub-cycle)
           • ultra-fine (phase-level)

EXAMPLE: TEMPORAL INSTRUMENT SET
       For a 1:1 harmonic epoch:

           • harmonic-phase meter: φ_harm stability = 0.98
           • resonance chronometer: R_peak decay = 0.03/cycle
           • ancestry chronograph: continuity = 0.91

       Interpretation:
           • stable epoch
           • low drift
           • high sweep viability

RESONANCE-DECAY HALF-LIVES
       Resonance-decay half-lives measure how long it takes for resonance
       amplitude or spectral density to decay by half.

       Half-life variables:
           • envelope amplitude R(t)
           • spectral density S(f,t)
           • gradient polarity G(t)
           • resonance-window width W_res

       Half-life types:

           amplitude half-life
                  Time for R_peak to fall to 50%.

           spectral half-life
                  Time for S(f,t) to broaden by 50%.

           gradient half-life
                  Time for polarity stability to fall by 50%.

           hybrid half-life
                  Weighted combination of all above.

       Half-lives indicate resonance stability across epochs.

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE-DECAY HALF-LIFE
       R_peak decay:
           Cycle 1: 1.0
           Cycle 8: 0.50

       Half-life:
           • 8 cycles
           • resonance epoch: mid-phase decay

HARMONIC-PHASE CHRONOMETRIC CALIBRATION
       Harmonic-phase calibration aligns chronometers to harmonic cycles.

       Calibration variables:
           • harmonic ratio n:m
           • phase alignment φ_harm
           • lock stability index L_harm
           • overtone/subharmonic structure

       Calibration types:

           integer calibration
                  1:1, 2:1, 3:1
                  → stable, long-duration calibration.

           rational calibration
                  3:2, 5:3, 7:4
                  → transitional calibration.

           nested calibration
                  Harmonics inside harmonics.

           resonance-harmonic calibration
                  Calibration aligned to resonance peaks.

       Calibration ensures chronometers remain synchronized across epochs.

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC-PHASE CALIBRATION
       Harmonic ratio: 3:2
       φ_harm drift: 0.04/cycle

       Calibration:
           • adjust chronometer by +0.04 per cycle
           • lock stability restored to 0.97

ANCESTRY-BASED CHRONOMETRIC STABILIZATION
       Sweep ancestry provides a stable reference frame for chronometric
       calibration.

       Stabilization variables:
           • ancestry continuity index
           • terrace thickness
           • fusion density
           • sweep timing drift

       Stabilization types:

           primary ancestry stabilization
                  Based on uninterrupted sweep lineage.

           laminated ancestry stabilization
                  Based on fine-scale sweep variation.

           tertiary ancestry stabilization
                  Based on deformed or eroded sweep fossils.

       Ancestry stabilization anchors harmonic and resonance chronometers.

EXAMPLE: ANCESTRY STABILIZATION
       Sweep ancestry continuity: 0.94
       Terrace thickness: 7 cycles

       Stabilization:
           • chronometer drift reduced by 60%
           • epoch boundary accuracy improved

CONSTELLATION-SCALE CHRONOMETRIC FUSION
       Chronometric fusion aligns temporal instruments across platforms.

       Fusion layers:
           1. resonance chronometer fusion
           2. harmonic-phase meter fusion
           3. ancestry chronograph fusion
           4. spectral drift gauge fusion
           5. epoch integrator fusion

       Fusion modes:
           • synchronous fusion
           • harmonic fusion
           • braided fusion
           • mesh fusion

       Fusion output:
           • constellation chronometric standard
           • unified epoch clock
           • constellation resonance half-life

CHRONOMETRY FAILURE MODES
       Chronometry may degrade due to:

           • chronometer drift
           • calibration loss
           • resonance half-life collapse
           • harmonic-phase instability
           • ancestry discontinuity

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • chronometry-integrity score
           • epoch-clock drift
           • half-life deviation
           • calibration error

       WRSADC halts unsafe chronometric states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • chronometer readings
           • half-life evolution
           • calibration history
           • epoch durations
           • fused chronometric sequences

       Lineage enables long-term chronometric forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • calibrates chronometers
           • computes resonance half-lives
           • aligns harmonic-phase meters
           • fuses constellation chronometry
           • blocks unsafe chronometric states

       WRSADC is the chronometry conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital chronometry transforms resonance-time into a precisely measurable
       temporal system. Precision instruments, resonance half-lives, and
       harmonic-phase calibration ensure that sweep choreography, harmonic-lock,
       and constellation-scale synchronization remain stable across long mission
       timelines.

